#!/bin/sh
# The "browser" an agent's own Playwright finds on claude-code-browser
# (BROWSER-ENFORCE, part of #1030; docs/design/live-browser.md §3.2).
#
# install-browser-stub.py puts a copy of this file at every path Playwright
# launches Chromium from under the agent's PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH, which the
# claude-code runner points at a stub directory whenever the live browser
# exists. So `p.chromium.launch()` and `launch_persistent_context()`, headless
# or not, start this instead: it says where the real browser is, on stderr --
# which Playwright puts in the launch error the agent reads -- and exits
# non-zero before Playwright can connect. A Chromium the agent started itself
# would be hidden from the person watching: task_7cda0d5335f049b4b442 did
# exactly that, and its work never appeared on the console.
#
# The gate's Chromium and the `browser` runner's keep /opt/playwright, the
# image's ENV; nothing here touches them.
cat >&2 <<'EOF'
This agent's browser is the live browser the person watches: connect with chromium.connect_over_cdp(os.environ['SWARM_CDP_ENDPOINT'])
In Python, `from swarm_browser import live; playwright, browser, context = live()` does that for you,
and the browser_navigate / browser_snapshot / browser_tabs tools already drive the same browser.
Starting a separate Chromium is refused here: nobody could see it.
EOF
exit 1
